73, "baseline_margin": 17.19, "analyst_adjustment": -0.19, "key_factors": ["Colorado's 6-point Democratic lean plus a favorable environment", "Incumbent Hickenlooper with a large fundraising edge", "All ten expert raters call the race Solid or Safe D", "Markets price Hickenlooper at 96-98%"], "rationale": "Hickenlooper is a well-funded incumbent in a state leaning Democratic, and every major rater places the race in the Safe/Solid D column while markets price his win at 96% or higher. Republican Mark Baisley has raised almost nothing, and independent Bob Chew's spending is unlikely to close a double-digit gap.", "watch": "A late surge of outside spending backing Bob Chew's well-funded independent bid could fragment the vote and shave the margin, though not the likely outcome.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent John Hickenlooper (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Senator John Hickenlooper enters the general election as a heavy favorite after fending off a progressive primary challenge from Julie Gonzales in June. Colorado leans Democratic by about six points, and the incumbent holds a massive financial advantage, having raised over $8 million to Republican Mark Baisley's roughly $138,000. Independent Bob Chew has self-funded a seven-figure campaign, but no outside groups are opposing Hickenlooper.\n\nEvery major expert rating places the race as Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets price Hickenlooper's win chances between 96 and 98 percent. With no public general-election polling available, the forecast rests on the state's partisan lean, the incumbent's advantages, and the unanimous expert consensus, all pointing to a comfortable Hickenlooper victory.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9917, "margin": 17.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9895, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9916, "margin": 17.0}]}